WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:05.680
Matthew Perry beat up a future Prime Minister,&nbsp;
got Bruce Willis to take a role on a bet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:09.600
and once had his heart stop for five&nbsp;
minutes. After Perry's tragic passing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.800
here's a look back at the most&nbsp;
fascinating stories from his life.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:17.680
It's hard to imagine Matthew Perry doing&nbsp;
anything other than making audiences laugh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:21.960
because he's been doing it for so long. He got&nbsp;
his start guest-starring on shows like "Charles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:26.760
in Charge" and "Silver Spoons," and a memorable&nbsp;
recurring role on "Growing Pains," and has since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:32.480
become synonymous with sitcoms. Believe it or not,&nbsp;
though, tennis was his first serious ambition.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.240
Perry had quite a promising tennis career earlier&nbsp;
in life. In his youth, Perry was a highly-ranked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:42.160
player on the junior circuit in Canada where he&nbsp;
grew up, and had real hopes of turning pro. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:47.600
move to Los Angeles changed things and gave him&nbsp;
some perspective. He told Men's Health in 2012.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.560
"Giving up tennis wasn't really&nbsp;
a decision I had to make. I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:53.800
a very good tennis player in Ottawa,&nbsp;
Canada — nationally ranked when I was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:59.000
like, 13. Then I moved to Los Angeles when&nbsp;
I was 15, and everyone in L.A. just killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:04.040
me. [...] I realized I wouldn't be playing&nbsp;
tennis for a living, so I went for acting."

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:07.920
In the 1990s, Matthew Perry wasn't&nbsp;
just one of the biggest stars on TV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:11.600
he was one of the biggest actors in&nbsp;
Hollywood, period. He and the rest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:15.320
the cast of "Friends" were bigger than just&nbsp;
about anyone, and that meant that everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:20.000
wanted to know who they were dating. Over the&nbsp;
years, a few of Perry's high-profile romances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:25.092
have been well-chronicled, including his time&nbsp;
dating "Baywatch" star Yasmine Bleeth in 1995.

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:28.240
"Aw, you're just saying that because&nbsp;
you're in love with Yasmine Bleeth."

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:30.678
"How could anyone not be in&nbsp;
love with Yasmine Bleeth?"

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:34.960
He also dated Neve Campbell a few years&nbsp;
later, Lizzy Caplan in the late 2000s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:40.160
and had a brief engagement to literary manager&nbsp;
Molly Hurwitz that was broken off in 2020.

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:43.680
What you may not recall are some of his&nbsp;
lesser-known loves, including "The Bold and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:48.320
the Beautiful" star Maeve Quinlan in 2002,&nbsp;
a very brief romance with "Gilmore Girls"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:53.920
star Lauren Graham a year later, and volleyball&nbsp;
player Rachel Dunn not long after. But even more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:58.120
lesser-known are some of the famous women he went&nbsp;
out with that never turned into relationships,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:03.480
such as an infamous terrible first date with&nbsp;
Cameron Diaz. Perry said in his memoir, "Friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:07.280
Lovers, and the Big Terrible Thing" that they&nbsp;
were set up on a date, which ended when Diaz&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:13.440
accidentally punched Perry in the face after he&nbsp;
told a joke during a game of Pictionary. Whoops!

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:17.400
Once he became a household name on "Friends"&nbsp;
it was another superstar that Perry pursued,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:22.840
beginning a relationship with superstar&nbsp;
Julia Roberts. As he described to GQ in 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:27.960
after the publication of his memoir, his&nbsp;
courtship of Roberts was "unbelievably romantic."

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:31.560
As Perry detailed, it all started when&nbsp;
he began chasing Roberts to be a guest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:35.520
star on "Friends." To his surprise,&nbsp;
the big screen star showed interest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:40.160
but only if her part would put her into a&nbsp;
storyline with Perry's Chandler Bing. Flattered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:45.080
Perry realized that Roberts might have an interest&nbsp;
in more than just a guest spot. He first tried to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:50.240
woo her with a traditional bouquet of roses, to&nbsp;
which Perry got an unusual response. She wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:55.520
him to concisely explain quantum physics to&nbsp;
her, and it wasn't a joke. Perry explained:

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:59.080
"The following day, I sent her a paper&nbsp;
all about wave-particle duality and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:03.200
uncertainty principle and entanglement,&nbsp;
and only some of it was metaphorical."

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.760
They began flirting heavily over fax of all&nbsp;
things, and before long Roberts not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:13.880
agreed to guest star, but she and Perry became a&nbsp;
couple before they even shot her guest appearance.

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:16.320
Like many stars and ordinary people alike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:19.800
Matthew Perry has had his struggles with&nbsp;
alcohol and drug addiction, which have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:24.520
well chronicled. The actor admits that during&nbsp;
production on "Friends," he was rarely sober,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:28.920
with his substance use so bad that he lived&nbsp;
in rehab during part of the show's run.

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:34.320
"You know, it's been pretty well documented that I&nbsp;
had some problems with alcoholism and addiction."

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:37.360
After years, though, Perry finally&nbsp;
focused on cleaning up his life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:40.280
and he spent $9 million to see it through.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:44.080
More than a dozen surgeries were required&nbsp;
to save Perry's life over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:47.120
including one that doctors told his&nbsp;
family he almost certainly wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:50.680
survive. Against all odds,&nbsp;
Perry came out the other side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:55.480
but to keep himself healthy, it cost him&nbsp;
millions of dollars. He wrote in his memoir

00:03:55.480 --> 00:04:00.480
"I have been to six thousand AA meetings.&nbsp;
I've been to rehab fifteen times. I've been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:06.040
in a mental institution, gone to therapy twice&nbsp;
a week for thirty years, been to death's door."

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:07.960
At one point, after his colon ruptured,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:12.160
Perry's problems were so bad they&nbsp;
left him in a coma. He told People:

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:16.200
"I was put on a thing called an ECMO&nbsp;
machine, which does all the breathing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:21.040
for your heart and your lungs. That's&nbsp;
called a Hail Mary. No one survives that."

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:27.160
But survive Perry did, and at the time of his&nbsp;
death, he'd been sober for nearly three years.

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:29.280
Few would ever think of Matthew Perry as a brute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:32.520
and certainly not the kind of person who&nbsp;
would go on national television telling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:38.400
stories about how he smacked around the Canadian&nbsp;
Prime Minister. Yet, in 2022, Perry did just that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:42.200
though the important context is that they&nbsp;
were in grade school together. Perry told&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:47.880
Jimmy Kimmel in 2017 that he and a friend were&nbsp;
jealous of Justin Trudeau and, well, beat him up.

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:51.240
"We actually beat up Justin Trudeau."

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:55.040
"You know I'm not bragging about this.&nbsp;
This is terrible. I was a stupid kid."

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:57.800
Kimmel pointed out that the incident&nbsp;
occurred when Trudeau's father was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:01.080
the country's sitting Prime Minister,&nbsp;
though it doesn't sound like Perry got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:04.520
into any more serious trouble than&nbsp;
your average playground tussler.

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:07.960
As for Trudeau himself, the current&nbsp;
Prime Minister responded playfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:11.680
challenging Perry to a rematch two weeks&nbsp;
after he'd made the remarks on Kimmel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:17.040
well-timed for April Fool's Day.&nbsp;
Perry humbly declined a second round.

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:21.240
"Friends" was a show full of big laughs&nbsp;
from every member of the cast. Each had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:24.560
unique style of humor, and Matthew&nbsp;
Perry was no exception. But some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:27.760
in the cast weren't always thrilled with&nbsp;
the jokes they were given in the script,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:31.680
either feeling they weren't funny or&nbsp;
didn't fit their character. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:35.280
playing these roles every week for years, the&nbsp;
performers may know their characters better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:40.720
than anyone. And when Perry wanted a different&nbsp;
joke, his tactic was to sabotage the script.

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:45.000
In her book, "End Credits: How I Broke Up With&nbsp;
Hollywood," series writer Patty Lin talked about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:49.200
how the likes of Perry, Jennifer Aniston, and&nbsp;
Courtney Cox would ruin jokes written in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:54.160
script by intentionally delivering them wrong or&nbsp;
badly. This would often force the crew to look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:59.840
for a fix, necessitating a rewrite, with the cast&nbsp;
themselves often pitching in ideas for new jokes.

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:03.200
The pushback on what they deemed as&nbsp;
a "bad joke" wasn't always helpful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:07.840
though — even if they might have made the show&nbsp;
better — especially to writers like Lin, who said:

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:09.160
"These sessions had a dire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:14.560
aggressive quality that lacked all the levity&nbsp;
you'd expect from the making of a sitcom."

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:18.280
Matthew Perry suffered numerous health&nbsp;
crises through the years, which are all well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:22.440
documented. One of the most horrific occurred&nbsp;
while he was doing another stint in rehab,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:27.720
and was preparing for surgery for reported stomach&nbsp;
pain, which required a dose of heavy anesthesia.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:32.720
According to Perry, the drug had actually stopped&nbsp;
his heart for five full minutes. He wrote:

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:36.440
"I was told that some beefy Swiss guy really&nbsp;
didn't want the guy from 'Friends' dying on his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:40.984
table and did CPR on me for the full five&nbsp;
minutes, beating and pounding my chest."

00:06:40.984 --> 00:06:47.344
"I got CPR, and the guy that&nbsp;
saved my life broke eight ribs."

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:51.560
The broken ribs required more physical&nbsp;
rehab. This became a problem for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:56.280
next movie because he'd just been cast in a&nbsp;
major role in Adam McKay's "Don't Look Up."

00:06:56.280 --> 00:07:00.120
As Perry recounts, he was initially to play&nbsp;
a Republican journalist who shares the screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:05.640
with Meryl Streep in a few scenes, and it&nbsp;
was "heartbreaking" to miss out on the role.

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:08.520
For some fans, Matthew Perry was&nbsp;
the heart and soul of "Friends,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:12.320
with his signature wisecracks becoming&nbsp;
one of the best things about the series.

00:07:12.476 --> 00:07:14.396
"You didn't cry when Bambi's mother died?"

00:07:14.396 --> 00:07:17.480
"Yes, it was very sad when the&nbsp;
guy stopped drawing the deer."

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:20.640
Believe it or not, though, Matthew Perry&nbsp;
almost missed out on the chance to join&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:24.760
the series in a leading role, not once&nbsp;
but twice. The first obstacle was that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:29.520
Perry was already committed to another project&nbsp;
when it came time to audition for the series.

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:36.840
"I was off the market because I had&nbsp;
taken a job on a pilot called "LAX 2194."

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:41.000
The series, a sci-fi comedy co-starring&nbsp;
Kelly Hu and Ryan Stiles, followed a group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:45.880
of baggage handlers working in the Los Angeles&nbsp;
International Airport in the distant future.

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:50.840
"In which I had to sort out the aliens'&nbsp;
luggage and that was basically the show."

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:53.960
The goofy series pilot thankfully&nbsp;
didn't impress network execs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:58.000
with one saying it was "the worst thing&nbsp;
we've ever seen in our lives." Suddenly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:02.400
Perry was available to audition, and the&nbsp;
rest is history ... or so many may think.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:06.200
But the truth is, Chandler — and Phoebe&nbsp;
— were originally intended to be minor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.969 --> 00:08:21.169
Elizabeth's father Paul, played by&nbsp;
"Die Hard" star Bruce Willis. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.169 --> 00:08:25.009
with the show's history of big-name guest&nbsp;
stars, Willis' appearance was a big deal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.009 --> 00:08:29.049
and an even bigger one because he&nbsp;
stuck around for three whole episodes.

00:08:29.049 --> 00:08:36.209
"You know they say that still waters&nbsp;
run deep, and I want to swim in yours."

00:08:36.209 --> 00:08:38.009
"You talkin' about having sex?"

00:08:38.009 --> 00:08:41.729
Apparently, Willis did it because he&nbsp;
lost a bet to Perry. He and Willis met&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.729 --> 00:08:45.129
during production of the film "The&nbsp;
Whole Nine Yards." During filming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.129 --> 00:08:49.369
the two actors bonded over their love of&nbsp;
partying between takes, but during their fun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.369 --> 00:08:53.329
Willis expressed concerns that the film&nbsp;
was going to flop. Perry disagreed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.329 --> 00:08:58.289
and the two made a wager: If the movie was a&nbsp;
hit, Willis had to do a guest spot on "Friends."

00:08:58.289 --> 00:09:02.329
Arriving in theaters in February 2000,&nbsp;
"The Whole Nine Yards" was #1 at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.329 --> 00:09:07.809
box office en route to a $106 million&nbsp;
haul, more than double its $41 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.809 --> 00:09:12.409
budget. Willis got the last laugh, though,&nbsp;
as in addition to three excellent episodes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.409 --> 00:09:17.169
Willis took home an Emmy Award for&nbsp;
his performance as Paul Stevens.

00:09:17.169 --> 00:09:20.649
With the triumph of "The Whole Nine Yards,"&nbsp;
Matthew Perry had a major movie win,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.649 --> 00:09:24.809
and with "Friends" coming to an end not long&nbsp;
after, the actor likely saw a path to a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.809 --> 00:09:29.169
career on the big screen. First up was a&nbsp;
sequel, "The Whole Ten Yards," reuniting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.169 --> 00:09:33.969
him with Bruce Willis for another run at box&nbsp;
office gold. But the follow-up was a major bomb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.969 --> 00:09:38.609
and it's one that made Perry completely&nbsp;
reassess his career. As he wrote in his memoir:

00:09:38.609 --> 00:09:42.969
"That was the moment Hollywood decided to&nbsp;
no longer invite Mr. Perry to be in movies."

00:09:42.969 --> 00:09:47.249
Forced to re-evaluate his career, Perry used&nbsp;
the failure of "The Whole Ten Yards" to pivot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.249 --> 00:09:51.329
looking for quality roles first and&nbsp;
foremost. One of his next projects was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.329 --> 00:09:56.169
the Aaron Sorkin-created TV series "Studio&nbsp;
60 on the Sunset Strip," which arrived with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.169 --> 00:09:59.969
major hype, with many thinking it&nbsp;
was going to be the next big hit.

00:09:59.969 --> 00:10:04.769
Famously overshadowed by the similarly-themed&nbsp;
"30 Rock" from Tina Fey, "Studio 60" was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.769 --> 00:10:09.169
canceled after just one year, but that&nbsp;
didn't stop Perry. Turning to writing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.169 --> 00:10:14.489
he created "Mr. Sunshine" in 2011 and "Go&nbsp;
On" in 2012. Neither lasted, but he made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.489 --> 00:10:19.591
a major comeback on "The Odd Couple" reboot&nbsp;
in 2015, which had a solid three-season run.

